== The Fear Of Final Falling

A #smallcaps[dark] fear haunts the minds of many who are coming to Christ; #emph[they are afraid that they will not persevere to the end]. I have heard the seeker say: “If I were to cast my soul on Jesus, perhaps I would still draw back to destruction after all. I have had good feelings before now, and they have died away. My goodness has been like the morning cloud, and like the early dew. It has come all of a sudden, lasted for a while, promised much, and then vanished away.”

My reader, I believe that this fear is often the father of the fact; and that some who have been afraid to trust Christ for all time, and for all eternity, have failed because they had a temporary faith, which never went far enough to save them. They set out trusting Jesus to some extent, but looking to themselves to keep going and to persevere on the way to heaven; and so they set out on the wrong footing, and, as a natural consequence, turned back before long. If we trust ourselves for our holding on, we will #emph[not] hold on. Even if we rest on Jesus for part of our salvation, we will fail if we trust self for anything. No chain is stronger than its weakest link: if Jesus is our hope for everything except one thing, we will utterly fail, because at that one point we will come to nothing. I have no doubt whatever that a mistake about the perseverance of the saints has prevented the perseverance of many who were running well. What hindered them from continuing to run? They trusted themselves for that running, and so they stopped short. Beware of mixing even a little of self into the mortar you build with, or you will make it poorly mixed mortar, and the stones will not hold together. If you look to Christ for your beginnings, beware of looking to yourself for your endings. He is Alpha. See to it that you make him Omega too. If you begin in the Spirit, you must not hope to be made perfect by the flesh. Begin as you mean to go on, and go on as you began, and let the Lord be all in all to you. Oh, that God the Holy Spirit may give us a very clear idea of where the strength must come from by which we will be preserved until the day of our Lord’s appearing!

Here is what Paul once said on this subject when he was writing to the Corinthians:

#quote[“Our Lord Jesus Christ, who will also confirm you#super[+] to the end, so that you will be blameless in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ. God is faithful, by whom you#super[+] were called into the fellowship of His Son Jesus Christ our Lord.” — 1 Cor 1:8, 9.]

This language quietly admits a great need, by telling us how it is met. Wherever the Lord makes a provision, we may be quite sure that there was a need for it, since nothing superfluous clutters the covenant of grace. Golden shields hung in Solomon’s courts which were never used, but there are none like them in the armory of God. What God has provided we will surely need. Between this hour and the end of all things, every promise of God and every provision of the covenant of grace will be called into use. The urgent need of the believing soul is confirmation, continuance, final perseverance, preservation to the end. #emph[This is the great need of the most advanced believers], for Paul was writing to saints at Corinth, who were men of a high order, of whom he could say, “I always thank my God for you#super[+] because of the grace He has given you in Christ Jesus.” Such men are the very people who most surely feel that they need new grace every day if they are to hold on, and hold out, and come off conquerors at the last. If you#super[+] were not saints you would have no grace, and you would feel no need of more grace; but because you are men of God, you feel the daily demands of the spiritual life. The marble statue needs no food; but the living man hungers and thirsts, and he rejoices that his bread and his water are made sure to him, for otherwise he would certainly faint on the way. The believer’s personal needs make it inevitable that he should draw daily from the great source of all supplies; for what could he do if he could not turn to his God?

#emph[This is true of the most gifted of the saints] — of those men at Corinth who were enriched in all speech and in all knowledge. They needed to be confirmed to the end, or else their gifts and attainments would prove their ruin. If we had the tongues of men and of angels, where would we be if we did not receive fresh grace? If we had every experience, till we were fathers in the church — if we had been taught by God so as to understand all mysteries — we still could not live a single day without the divine life flowing into us from our Covenant Head. How could we hope to hold on for a single hour, to say nothing of a lifetime, unless the Lord held us on? He who began the good work in us must complete it until the day of Christ, or it will prove a painful failure.

#emph[This great need arises very largely from ourselves]. Some have a painful fear that they will not persevere in grace, because they know their own fickleness. Certain people are unstable by temperament. Some men are by nature conservative, not to say obstinate; but others are just as naturally changeable and volatile. Like butterflies, they flit from flower to flower, till they have visited all the beauties of the garden, and settle on none of them. They never stay long enough in one place to do any good; not even in their business nor in their intellectual pursuits. Such people may well be afraid that ten, twenty, thirty, forty, perhaps fifty years of continual religious watchfulness will be far too much for them. We see men joining first one church and then another, till they have gone all around the compass. They are everything by turns and nothing for long. Such people have double need to pray that they may be confirmed by God, and made not only steadfast but immovable, or else they will not be found “always abounding in the work of the Lord.”

All of us, even if we have no temptation to fickleness in our temperament, must feel our own weakness if we have really been made alive by God. Dear reader, do you not find enough in any single day to make you stumble? You#super[+] who desire to walk in perfect holiness, as I trust you do; you who have set before you a high standard of what a Christian should be — do you not find that before the breakfast things are cleared from the table, you have shown enough folly to make you ashamed of yourselves? If we were to shut ourselves up in a hermit’s lonely cell, temptation would follow us; for as long as we cannot escape from ourselves, we cannot escape from incitements to sin. There is something in our hearts which should make us watchful and humble before God. If he does not confirm us, we are so weak that we will stumble and fall; not overturned by an enemy, but by our own carelessness. Lord, be our strength. We are weakness itself.

Besides that, #emph[there is the weariness which comes from a long life]. When we first profess our faith in Christ, we mount up with wings like eagles; further on we run without growing weary; but in our best and truest days we walk without fainting. Our pace seems slower, but it is more useful and better sustained. I pray God that the energy of our youth may stay with us, so far as it is the energy of the Spirit and not the mere fermentation of proud flesh. He who has long been on the road to heaven finds that there was good reason why it was promised that his shoes would be iron and brass, for the road is rough. He has found out that there are Hills of Difficulty and Valleys of Humiliation; that there is a Vale of Deathshade, and, worse still, a Vanity Fair — and all these must be passed through. If there are Delectable Mountains (and, thank God, there are), there are also Castles of Despair, whose insides pilgrims have too often seen. All things considered, those who hold out to the end in the way of holiness will be “men wondered at.”

“O world of wonders, I can say no less.” The days of a Christian’s life are like so many Koh-i-noor diamonds of mercy threaded on the golden string of divine faithfulness. In heaven we will tell angels, and principalities, and powers of the unsearchable riches of Christ which were spent on us, and enjoyed by us while we were here below. We have been kept alive on the brink of death. Our spiritual life has been a flame burning on in the middle of the sea, a stone that has stayed hanging in the air. It will amaze the universe to see us enter the pearly gate, blameless in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ. We ought to be full of grateful wonder if we are kept for an hour; and I trust we are.

If this were all, there would be cause enough for anxiety; but there is far more. #emph[We have to think of what a place we live in]. The world is a howling wilderness to many of God’s people. Some of us are greatly favored in the providence of God, but others have a hard fight of it. We begin our day with prayer, and we hear the sound of holy song very often in our homes; but many good people have hardly risen from their knees in the morning before they are greeted with blasphemy. They go out to work, and all day long they are vexed with filthy conversation, like righteous Lot in Sodom. Can you even walk the open streets these days without your ears being afflicted by foul language? The world is no friend to grace. The best we can do with this world is to get through it as quickly as we can, for we live in an enemy’s country. A robber lurks in every bush. Everywhere we need to travel with a drawn sword in our hand, or at least with that weapon which is called #emph[all-prayer] always at our side; for we have to fight for every inch of our way. Make no mistake about this, or you will be roughly shaken out of your fond delusion. O God, help us, and confirm us to the end, or where will we be?

True religion is supernatural at its beginning, supernatural in its continuance, and supernatural at its close. It is the work of God from first to last. There is great need that the hand of the Lord should still be stretched out: my reader is feeling that need now, and I am glad that he feels it; for now he will look for his own preservation to the Lord, who alone is able to keep us from falling, and to glorify us with his Son.
